</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Continuous Diffusion Model</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Latent Diffusion Process.</span>
Diffusion models generate data by learning to reverse a fixed forward noising process. Given an initial latent variable <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}\sim p_{\text{data}}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}\sim p_{\text{data}}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, the forward process incrementally perturbs <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> into Gaussian noise through a Markov chain:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(x_{t}\mid x_{t-1})=\mathcal{N}\!\left(x_{t};\sqrt{1-\beta_{t}}\,x_{t-1},\beta_{t}I\right),\ t\in[1,T]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.798em;" width="0.798em"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(x_{t}\mid x_{t-1})=\mathcal{N}\!\left(x_{t};\sqrt{1-\beta_{t}}\,x_{t-1},\beta_{t}I\right),\ t\in[1,T],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\beta_{t}\}_{t=1}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\beta_{t}\}_{t=1}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is a predefined noise schedule. The diffusion model learns a parameterized reverse process:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}(x_{t-1}\mid x_{t})\approx q(x_{t-1}\mid x_{t},x_{0})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}(x_{t-1}\mid x_{t})\approx q(x_{t-1}\mid x_{t},x_{0}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">which is optimized using a reweighted denoising score-matching loss. In practice, the model predicts either the original clean input <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, the injected noise <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, or the velocity <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Salimans and Ho (2022)</a></cite>, and training reduces to a simple regression objective.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Text-to-Continuous Mapping.</span>
Since diffusion models operate in continuous spaces while text is inherently discrete, we first map input sentences into continuous latent representations. We adopt an architecture with an encoder <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> and a decoder <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, instantiated as a variational autoencoder (VAE) model, where the encoder maps input text <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> to latent representation <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=E(x)\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=E(x)\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the decoder reconstructs text from latents <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}=D(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}=D(z)</annotation></semantics></math>. The model is initialized from a pretrained language model and fine-tuned on reconstruction tasks (training details in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Method ‣ Controllable Stylistic Text Generation with Train-Time Attribute-Regularized Diffusion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). Once trained, the encoder and decoder are frozen, providing a stable continuous representation space for the subsequent diffusion process.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Classifier Guidance.</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dhariwal and Nichol (2021)</a></cite> introduces classifier guidance, which augments the diffusion score with the gradient of an auxiliary classifier’s log-likelihood. At inference time, the diffusion score is modified to include the gradient of the log-likelihood of an auxiliary classifier as:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\epsilon}_{\theta}(x_{t},t,c)=\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t,c)-\gamma\,\sigma_{t}\,\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{\phi}(c\mid x_{t})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\epsilon}_{\theta}(x_{t},t,c)=\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t,c)-\gamma\,\sigma_{t}\,\nabla_{x_{t}}\log p_{\phi}(c\mid x_{t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t,c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t,c)</annotation></semantics></math> is the conditional noise prediction, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\phi}(c\mid x_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\phi}(c\mid x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is the auxiliary classifier evaluated on the noisy sample <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the noise level (standard deviation) at step <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> controls the guidance strength.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Classifier-free Guidance.</span>
A central technique used in this work is classifier-free guidance (CFG) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho and Salimans (2022)</a></cite>. During the training of the diffusion model, both conditional and unconditional denoising objectives are optimized jointly. At inference time, the two predictions are combined as:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\hat{\epsilon}_{\theta}(x_{t},t,c)=(1+\gamma)\,\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t,c)-\gamma\,\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t,\varnothing),}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\hat{\epsilon}_{\theta}(x_{t},t,c)=(1+\gamma)\,\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t,c)-\gamma\,\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t,\varnothing),}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t,c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t,c)</annotation></semantics></math> is the noise prediction conditioned on attribute <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t,\varnothing)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon_{\theta}(x_{t},t,\varnothing)</annotation></semantics></math> is the unconditional prediction, and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> controls the strength of guidance.
By interpolating between the two paths, CFG amplifies attribute information while retaining the semantic content of the original input.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F2.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:69.0pt;"><img src="2510.06386v1/figures/1_sentiment_paralle_no_cluster_z0.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:96/84;" width="96" height="84" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Sentiment data without inductive bias.</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F2.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:69.0pt;"><img src="2510.06386v1/figures/2_formality_no_cluster_z0.png" id="S3.F2.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:96/82;" width="96" height="82" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Formality data without inductive bias.</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Method</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on previous findings, we introduce <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RegDiff</span> (Attribute-<span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reg</span>ularized <span id="S4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diff</span>usion) (Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4 Method ‣ Controllable Stylistic Text Generation with Train-Time Attribute-Regularized Diffusion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). The framework integrates a variational autoencoder (VAE) with a diffusion model. After fine-tuning, the VAE encoder and decoder are frozen to provide stable latent representations. The encoder maps input texts into latent variables <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> (with <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{tgt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{tgt}</annotation></semantics></math> as inputs to the diffusion model and <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{src}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{src}</annotation></semantics></math> as condition for diffusion. During sampling, the diffusion model generates latent codes <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{z^{tgt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{z^{tgt}}</annotation></semantics></math> approximating <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{tgt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{tgt}</annotation></semantics></math>, which are then decoded into natural language via the frozen VAE decoder.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.06386v1/figures/5_latentfeadiff.drawio.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:224/380;" width="224" height="380" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>A graphical representation of the RegDiff framework.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>VAE model</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We adopt a general encoder–decoder architecture, where the encoder and decoder are jointly trained with an auxiliary classifier. In this architecture, we adopt VAE mode, but is not limited to VAE. In contrast to the conventional VAE objective of reconstruction loss plus KL divergence, we additionally include a classification loss to impose attribute bias. The encoder produces latent representations <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times S\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times S\times L}</annotation></semantics></math>. To obtain an attribute-specific representation, we apply a mean pooling operation <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> over the sequence dimension <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> to produce <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{z}</annotation></semantics></math>, consistent with standard practice in fine-tuning pretrained models for classification. The pooled vector <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{z}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{z}\in\mathbb{R}^{B\times L}</annotation></semantics></math> serves as input to the classifier, enabling both (i) classifier training for attribute prediction and (ii) gradient-based biasing of <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> such that attributes become separable at the pooled representation level (see Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.1 VAE model ‣ 4 Method ‣ Controllable Stylistic Text Generation with Train-Time Attribute-Regularized Diffusion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{z}=P(z)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{z}=P(z)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{vae}=L_{recon}+\alpha L_{KL}+\beta L_{classifier}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{vae}=L_{recon}+\alpha L_{KL}+\beta L_{classifier}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<div id="S4.F4.2" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:69.0pt;"><img src="2510.06386v1/figures/3_formality_biased.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:96/83;" width="96" height="83" alt="Refer to caption">
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<div id="S4.F4.3" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:69.0pt;"><img src="2510.06386v1/figures/4_authorship_biased.png" id="S4.F4.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:96/83;" width="96" height="83" alt="Refer to caption">
</div></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>The two figures represent: Inductive biased formality clusters and inductive biased authorship clusters.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Diffusion Model</h3>

<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Input.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Once the VAE is trained, we freeze its parameters and employ the encoder to obtain latent representations <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> from text inputs. Among the information encoded in these representations, we focus on two aspects: (1) token-level latent representation <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> that capture textual content, and (2) attribute-level clusters <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{z}</annotation></semantics></math> in a secondary manifold reflecting style.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In our task, the diffusion model employs classifier-free guidance, where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{tgt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{tgt}</annotation></semantics></math> serves as the diffusion input and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{src}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{src}</annotation></semantics></math> is used as a conditioning signal to constrain the semantic content during the denoising process of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{tgt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{tgt}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Training-time Regularization.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to introducing style attribute bias into the data, we incorporate a regularization loss during the diffusion model’s training to better align the distribution of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{z}</annotation></semantics></math> with that observed during training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, we introduce an auxiliary distributional matching loss in the attribute space:</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{attr}}=\mathcal{D}\!\left(p_{\theta}(P(\bar{z}))\,\|\,q(P(\bar{z}))\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>attr</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mpadded style="width:0.629em;" width="0.629em"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{attr}}=\mathcal{D}\!\left(p_{\theta}(P(\bar{z}))\,\|\,q(P(\bar{z}))\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a distribution similarity. In our task, we reuse the classifier which is jointly trained with VAE model. This classifier is also frozen, delivering to <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{pred}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{pred}</annotation></semantics></math> the gradients generated by the dissimilarity between <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{z}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{z}_{pred}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{z}_{pred}</annotation></semantics></math>. The overall training objective is then</p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{diffusion}}+\lambda\,\mathcal{L}_{\text{classifier}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>diffusion</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>classifier</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{diffusion}}+\lambda\,\mathcal{L}_{\text{classifier}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{classifier}}=\mathrm{CE}(\bar{z}_{pred},L)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>classifier</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>CE</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{classifier}}=\mathrm{CE}(\bar{z}_{pred},L)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Concretely, let <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the noisy latent at step <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(z_{t},t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(z_{t},t)</annotation></semantics></math> the network prediction under
the velocity parameterization (i.e., predicting the linear combination
of noise and clean latent). The standard diffusion loss is given by</p>
<table id="S4.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{diffusion}}=\mathbb{E}_{t,z_{0},\boldsymbol{\epsilon}}\big\|\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(z_{t},t)-\mathbf{v}\big\|^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>diffusion</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{diffusion}}=\mathbb{E}_{t,z_{0},\boldsymbol{\epsilon}}\big\|\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(z_{t},t)-\mathbf{v}\big\|^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the target velocity
corresponding to the ground-truth noise <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{tgt}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{tgt}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. These three concept can be converted into one another. As we need to calculate the regularization loss on <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{z}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can obtain the <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{z}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{z}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{v}</annotation></semantics></math>. The pooling method <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is differentiable, and the gradients of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{z}_{pred}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{z}_{pred}</annotation></semantics></math> can be passed to <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{pred}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{pred}</annotation></semantics></math> to influence the generation process.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{z}_{0}=\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\,z_{t}-\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\,\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(z_{t},t)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐯</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{z}_{0}=\sqrt{\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\,z_{t}-\sqrt{1-\bar{\alpha}_{t}}\,\mathbf{v}_{\theta}(z_{t},t),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The final objective combines the two terms following formula <a href="#S4.E6" title="In Training-time Regularization. ‣ 4.2 Diffusion Model ‣ 4 Method ‣ Controllable Stylistic Text Generation with Train-Time Attribute-Regularized Diffusion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>
where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> balances between reconstruction fidelity
and attribute preservation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiment</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Dataset</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our framework on five text style transfer datasets covering diverse stylistic attributes: Sentiment (parallel), Sentiment (non-parallel), Toxicity, Formality, and Authorship, based on Yelp <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen et al. (2017)</a></cite>, ParaDetox <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Logacheva et al. (2022)</a></cite>, GYAFC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rao and Tetreault (2018)</a></cite>, and Shakespeare <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2012)</a></cite>. The Sentiment (parallel) dataset is a synthetic corpus constructed by prompting Llama3 to generate sentiment-opposite rewrites of sentences from Yelp, forming aligned positive–negative pairs. A human sampling check was conducted to ensure the correctness and fluency of generated pairs (Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Llama3 generated Sentiment(parallel) dataset ‣ Controllable Stylistic Text Generation with Train-Time Attribute-Regularized Diffusion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>). Statistics of the data splits used for both VAE+Classifier joint training and RegDiff training are summarized in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Dataset Splits for Training ‣ Controllable Stylistic Text Generation with Train-Time Attribute-Regularized Diffusion" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S5.T2.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:149.5pt;vertical-align:-73.4pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-151.6pt,65.7pt) scale(0.532322019939662,0.532322019939662) ;">
<table id="S5.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S5.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.6pt 3.5pt;"></td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.6pt 3.5pt;" colspan="2"><span id="S5.T2.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sentiment(parallel)</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.6pt 3.5pt;" colspan="2"><span id="S5.T2.2.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sentiment (non-parallel)</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.6pt 3.5pt;" colspan="2"><span id="S5.T2.2.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Toxicity</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.6pt 3.5pt;" colspan="2"><span id="S5.T2.2.1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Formality</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.1.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.6pt 3.5pt;" colspan="2"><span id="S5.T2.2.1.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Authorship</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T2.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T2.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding:0.6pt 3.5pt;"><span id="S5.T2.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Setting / Direction</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.6pt 3.5pt;">(Pos→Neg)</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.6pt 3.5pt;">(Neg→Pos)</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.6pt 3.5pt;">(Pos→Neg)</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.6pt 3.5pt;">(Neg→Pos)</td>
<td id="S5.T2.2.1.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.6pt 3.5pt;">(Neu→Tox)</td>
